  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Field Notebook",
    "overview": "outflow records observations, not verdicts. The person's own history is the baseline: a charge is \"unusual\" because it's unusual *for them*, never because it broke a budget. The interface reads like a well-kept notebook opened at a desk in the evening: a dark, calm page, dense rows of dated entries with tabular figures, and an annotation in the margin only where something deserves a second look.\n\nDensity is high and type is small, because the main reader sits at a desktop and wants many rows in view. Depth comes from tonal steps between near-black navy surfaces and hairline borders, not from shadows. Colour is an ink used for meaning: money in, money out, the kind of account, and things that need attention. The chrome around the data stays neutral so the figures carry the page.\n\nControls are tactile and clear. They sit quietly at rest but always look pressable: a visible border, a real hit area, and a distinct pressed and focused state.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Dark, single theme (`color-scheme: dark`); near-black navy page with slightly lifted panels.",
      "System sans-serif throughout; all figures use tabular numerals.",
      "One interactive accent (Field Teal); every other hue encodes data meaning.",
      "Flat: hairline borders and tonal layering, no drop shadows on content.",
      "Narrow reading column (940px max) centred on the page."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Meaning-Only Rule",
        "body": "Every hue other than Field Teal encodes data meaning (in, out, account kind, attention). Chrome, layout and decoration stay on the neutral ramp. If a colour doesn't answer \"what kind of money, or does this need me?\", it shouldn't be there.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Not-Colour-Alone Rule",
        "body": "In versus out is never told by green versus red alone. The sign, a label or the lane always carries it as well.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "Every money figure uses `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` and right-aligns in columns, so digits line up down the page.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hairline Rule",
        "body": "Surfaces are separated by a 1px Hairline border or a tonal step, never by a shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do use the `--lg-*` custom properties in `app/src/ledger.css` for every new colour; they are the live token set.",
      "Do keep money figures tabular and right-aligned.",
      "Do give every control a visible border and a pressed and focused state, so it reads as pressable on the dark page.",
      "Do put one Key button per action group, at most.",
      "Do mirror token values into constants when SVG presentation attributes can't read CSS variables (as `Balances.tsx` does), and keep them in sync."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use the legacy `theme.css` palette (`--bg`, `--card`, `--accent`, the teal-to-blue gradient) for new work. Only the auth gate, toast and a few fallbacks still read it.",
      "Don't add drop shadows to content surfaces.",
      "Don't use colour for decoration; see the Meaning-Only Rule.",
      "Don't signal in versus out by colour alone.",
      "Don't put destructive or upkeep actions on an analysis screen; they belong in Settings."
    ]
  }
}